  <h2 id="money-check">Money check before you pay</h2>

  <p>
    Our strongest suggestion: spend about a tenth of your family education budget on exam preparation and credential checks. Going past a fifth is a warning sign, and it needs a clear reason. None of this applies to free or very cheap online learning, so start with the regulator's own guides and low-priced practice material.
  </p>

  <p>
    An illustration, not a real fee: if your family set aside Rs 4 lakh for your whole education and early career, a tenth is Rs 40,000 and a fifth is Rs 80,000. Keep the rest for living costs during the switch, travel, and a reserve in case the offer is delayed.
  </p>

  <p>Much exam content is free, so pay only for something free material cannot give you, such as marked mock tests or feedback from a nurse who passed. Judge the course, not the brand:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Who teaches it, and have they worked in the country you are targeting?</li>
    <li>When was the material last updated against the regulator's current exam?</li>
    <li>Can you see pass records, a demo class and the refund terms in writing?</li>
    <li>Does it end with something you can show, such as a practice log or mock results?</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    General information, not financial advice; get all terms in writing and do not borrow to pay an agent. Apply on the regulator or employer website, because no agent can promise a licence, a job or a visa. Our guide on <a href="/blog/study-abroad/education-loan-for-study-abroad-india/">education loans for study abroad</a> explains why repayment that depends on a foreign job is high risk.
  </p>

